WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.722
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online Newspaper Class, level three.

2
00:00:06.022 --> 00:00:09.594
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:09.894 --> 00:00:16.238
Unit nine: Local Government. Today you will write formal notices with will be + -ing and is scheduled to.

4
00:00:16.538 --> 00:00:19.225
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:20.167 --> 00:00:23.550
Part one: words. You will learn eight new words.

6
00:00:24.417 --> 00:00:25.638
City council.

7
00:00:26.650 --> 00:00:29.799
City council means the elected group that governs a city.

8
00:00:30.383 --> 00:00:32.673
The city council approved the new park.

9
00:00:33.283 --> 00:00:36.063
Your turn. Say: City council.

10
00:00:38.900 --> 00:00:40.319
City council.

11
00:00:41.083 --> 00:00:42.444
Municipal budget.

12
00:00:43.450 --> 00:00:47.214
Municipal budget means a city's yearly plan for income and spending.

13
00:00:47.817 --> 00:00:50.629
The municipal budget includes funds for roads.

14
00:00:51.217 --> 00:00:54.194
Your turn. Say: Municipal budget.

15
00:00:57.033 --> 00:00:58.592
Municipal budget.

16
00:00:59.350 --> 00:01:00.839
Public services.

17
00:01:01.850 --> 00:01:06.299
Public services means services such as transport, water and waste collection.

18
00:01:06.883 --> 00:01:09.045
Public services need steady funding.

19
00:01:09.650 --> 00:01:12.860
Your turn. Say: Public services.

20
00:01:15.700 --> 00:01:17.154
Public services.

21
00:01:17.917 --> 00:01:18.999
Zoning law.

22
00:01:19.983 --> 00:01:23.806
Zoning law means a rule about how land and buildings may be used.

23
00:01:24.417 --> 00:01:26.892
A zoning law banned factories near homes.

24
00:01:27.483 --> 00:01:29.973
Your turn. Say: Zoning law.

25
00:01:32.833 --> 00:01:33.904
Zoning law.

26
00:01:34.650 --> 00:01:35.895
Local election.

27
00:01:36.883 --> 00:01:40.938
Local election means a vote to choose a town or city's representatives.

28
00:01:41.550 --> 00:01:44.002
A local election often has low turnout.

29
00:01:44.617 --> 00:01:47.548
Your turn. Say: Local election.

30
00:01:50.400 --> 00:01:51.657
Local election.

31
00:01:52.417 --> 00:01:53.743
Civic engagement.

32
00:01:54.750 --> 00:01:58.561
Civic engagement means taking part in community and political life.

33
00:01:59.150 --> 00:02:01.602
Civic engagement strengthens neighbourhoods.

34
00:02:02.217 --> 00:02:05.194
Your turn. Say: Civic engagement.

35
00:02:08.033 --> 00:02:09.510
Civic engagement.

36
00:02:10.250 --> 00:02:11.599
Community project.

37
00:02:12.583 --> 00:02:16.951
Community project means a local plan carried out for the benefit of residents.

38
00:02:17.550 --> 00:02:19.991
The community project rebuilt the playground.

39
00:02:20.583 --> 00:02:24.408
Your turn. Say: Community project.

40
00:02:27.267 --> 00:02:28.616
Community project.

41
00:02:29.350 --> 00:02:30.444
Public hearing.

42
00:02:31.450 --> 00:02:35.330
Public hearing means an official meeting where citizens can speak about a plan.

43
00:02:35.917 --> 00:02:38.079
Residents spoke at the public hearing.

44
00:02:38.683 --> 00:02:41.823
Your turn. Say: Public hearing.

45
00:02:44.667 --> 00:02:46.062
Public hearing.

46
00:02:46.867 --> 00:02:50.285
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

47
00:02:51.150 --> 00:02:56.798
Visal, a notice says 'The council will be holding a public hearing on Monday.' Why not just 'will hold'?

48
00:02:57.883 --> 00:03:13.878
The future continuous sounds more polite and describes a planned, ordinary event. It is common in notices: will be holding, will be reviewing, will be publishing. It does not sound like a sudden decision.

49
00:03:14.983 --> 00:03:16.774
What other forms appear?

50
00:03:17.883 --> 00:03:29.197
Is scheduled to, is due to, and is to: 'The city council is scheduled to vote on the municipal budget.' These show the plan is official and the date is fixed.

51
00:03:30.283 --> 00:03:31.923
Can citizens use them too?

52
00:03:33.017 --> 00:03:43.587
Yes, in letters to the council. 'We will be attending the hearing' is clear and respectful, and good civic engagement starts with polite, precise messages.

53
00:03:44.733 --> 00:03:46.245
Part three: grammar.

54
00:03:47.083 --> 00:03:52.438
Grammar focus: Formal notices: will be plus -ing and is scheduled to.

55
00:03:52.738 --> 00:03:54.053
Look at the pattern.

56
00:03:54.750 --> 00:03:57.876
The city council will be holding a public hearing on Monday.

57
00:03:58.783 --> 00:04:01.909
The council is scheduled to vote on the municipal budget.

58
00:04:02.817 --> 00:04:05.733
Residents will be reviewing the zoning law proposals.

59
00:04:06.700 --> 00:04:13.541
Use will be + -ing for planned events in polite notices, and is scheduled to / is due to + verb for fixed official dates.

60
00:04:14.283 --> 00:04:18.248
Be careful! Don't say: The council will be hold a hearing.

61
00:04:20.200 --> 00:04:23.221
Say: The council will be holding a hearing.

62
00:04:23.521 --> 00:04:25.080
Use -ing after will be.

63
00:04:25.867 --> 00:04:28.415
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

64
00:04:29.200 --> 00:04:30.758
Notice from the Town Hall.

65
00:04:31.058 --> 00:04:35.659
Local government shapes daily life more directly than national politics.

66
00:04:36.667 --> 00:04:44.111
The city council will be discussing the municipal budget, and public services such as buses and waste collection are scheduled to be reviewed.

67
00:04:45.100 --> 00:04:50.583
A public hearing is due to take place before any zoning law changes, and residents will be sharing their views.

68
00:04:51.567 --> 00:04:56.933
Civic engagement matters most in a local election, when a few hundred votes can decide a result.

69
00:04:57.933 --> 00:05:02.058
A community project often begins with a single neighbour who attends the meeting.

70
00:05:03.167 --> 00:05:07.343
Part five: quiz. Five questions. Are you ready?

71
00:05:08.183 --> 00:05:10.415
The council blank a hearing on Monday.

72
00:05:10.715 --> 00:05:14.636
A, will be holding. B, is hold. C, will being held.

73
00:05:18.200 --> 00:05:20.200
The answer is A: will be holding.

74
00:05:21.350 --> 00:05:24.394
Rules about how land can be used are a blank law.

75
00:05:24.694 --> 00:05:27.860
A, hearing. B, zoning. C, budget.

76
00:05:31.400 --> 00:05:33.562
The answer is B: zoning.

77
00:05:34.717 --> 00:05:37.181
The council is scheduled blank on the budget.

78
00:05:37.481 --> 00:05:40.356
A, vote. B, voting. C, to vote.

79
00:05:43.900 --> 00:05:45.598
The answer is C: to vote.

80
00:05:46.750 --> 00:05:48.552
Choose the most formal notice.

81
00:05:48.852 --> 00:05:54.375
A, We'll meet Monday.. B, The council will be meeting on Monday.. C, Council meet Monday..

82
00:05:57.933 --> 00:06:00.955
The answer is B: The council will be meeting on Monday..

83
00:06:02.117 --> 00:06:06.322
According to the reading, civic engagement matters most in a blank election.

84
00:06:06.622 --> 00:06:10.043
A, local. B, foreign. C, school.

85
00:06:13.600 --> 00:06:15.460
The answer is A: local.

86
00:06:16.667 --> 00:06:19.749
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

87
00:06:20.583 --> 00:06:24.173
What local government decision has directly affected your daily life?

88
00:06:32.983 --> 00:06:34.437
Now listen to Visal.

89
00:06:34.737 --> 00:07:00.960
One local decision that affects daily life is where the council places bus routes or repairs roads. A new route can shorten a journey to school or work, while poor repairs make travel slower and less safe. Decisions about markets, parks and waste collection also shape how clean and convenient a neighbourhood is, so local government matters a great deal.

90
00:07:01.260 --> 00:07:03.097
Excellent answer, Visal!

91
00:07:03.983 --> 00:07:10.212
Great work! Now you can use eight new words and formal notices: will be plus -ing and is scheduled to.

92
00:07:10.512 --> 00:07:16.809
Today's value is community awareness. Notice how changes affect every neighbour, not only the winners.

93
00:07:17.109 --> 00:07:19.715
See you in Unit ten. Goodbye!

94
00:07:20.015 --> 00:07:22.958
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

